course: dictionary
lesson: 2451
title: A mild word: dang
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are a softer way of saying a stronger word. These softer words are called euphemisms, and people use them so they do not sound rude. «"Dang" is a mild word used instead of "damn"; it is American.» Mild means not strong, so dang is a gentler word.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Someone who drops a cup and says «Dang it!» is cross or surprised, but the word is soft, not rude. Ask yourself: does this word feel strong or gentle? Dang feels gentle.
explain: Dang has a second job too. In American speech, dang can mean "very", as in «dang good», which means very good. Check the words after it: if it comes before an adjective like good or big, it probably means "very".
explain: You will hear dang most in American speech, especially in the South and in the countryside. You will also hear it in cartoons and westerns. When you meet it, try the meaning in the sentence: "That was dang fast" still makes sense with "very" in place of dang.
fact: «Dang» is a mild word used instead of "damn".
fact: A euphemism is a softer word used in place of a stronger one.
fact: «Dang» is American, and it is especially common in the South and the countryside.
fact: As an adverb, «dang» can mean "very", as in «dang good».
fact: «Dang» shows annoyance or surprise, and it is polite enough for children.
exercise: «Dang» is a mild stand-in for another word. Which word does it replace? | Danger | Dinner | *Damn | Dance
exercise: Max drops his ice cream and shouts, «Dang it!» What does his word show? | Happiness about the beach | Hunger | A wish to go to bed | *Annoyance or surprise
exercise: In «that was dang good», what does dang mean? | Never | Dangerous | *Very | Sleepy
test: Why is «dang» called a euphemism? | It is a long word from Scotland | *It is a softer word used instead of a stronger one | It means a big dog | Nobody knows why
test: Where is «dang» most common? | In Australian news | *In American speech | In Scottish poems | In Japanese cartoons
exam: What does «dang» mean in «a dang fine day»? | Dinner | Dangerous | *Very | Sorry
